## Scope

Port `src/ranking/mod.rs` (398 lines) and load the six trained Naive-Bayes classifier JSONs as-is. `en_xx.json` is 167 KB; the six total 468 KB.

Ranking is **independent of the rules** — it scores whatever the engine produced. That is why it is broken out of #<%= spine_issue %> rather than bundled with the spine: once the base classes land, this can run alongside the whole of Wave A.

## Why it is not optional

Without ranking, overlapping-rule cases resolve differently and time parity is unreachable. It is not a tie-break nicety; it decides which of several competing parses wins.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `src/ranking/mod.rs`'s scoring ported to Ruby
- [ ] The six upstream trained classifier JSONs load as-is — **not retrained**; `ranking/train.rs` is explicitly out of scope per #<%= parent %>
- [ ] Classifier data vendored with its BSD-3-Clause notice preserved, as the corpus fixture is
- [ ] A test pins the ranker's output ordering on a case with overlapping rule matches, against the native backend's ordering
- [ ] The `require`-time delta of loading 468 KB of JSON is recorded, so the reduce stage can attribute it

## Files you own

- `lib/duckling/ruby/ranking.rb` (or a small directory under it)
- the vendored classifier JSONs
- `test/ruby_backend/ranking_test.rb`

